newReturnT -- SEQ 508 / 509 (Tony, 2026-11-02): rung 5, part 2 -- return on the new road. PRE-REGISTERED ON TRUNK, born red.

One process. Every action comes in a pair with the SAME body: the n action is built on the NEW road (nlNew: compileIn
with KantGrammar's STARt), the o action on the OLD road (nlOld: compileIn with no rule) -- the control. Each block of output
starts with a line RT <row> <road>; pop.sh reads the lines up to the next RT line. Every multi-line body is written with
indentation, never braces (fixit braceInCode). The file ends with RT END and a sentinel on stderr, so a define that breaks
shows as a missing sentinel rather than as missing values.

The caller resets rx to 55 before each call, so a call that hands back nothing leaves 55 -- a value no body here produces.

ROWS
RP    road proof (newLocalT's): the roads bind #5d in opposite directions -- new [ 7 ], old [     7 ]. And pop.sh asserts
      NO `parse failed` on stderr: a new-road build that fails falls back to the OLD road at the first call, silently,
      and its row would read as a green new road. (A call with EMPTY parens, `f();`, does not build on the new road --
      measured SEQ 509 -- so every call inside a body here passes an argument.)
RT-1  return 7 from a plain action: rx reads 7.
RT-2  return mid-body: rx reads 3 and the print after the return never runs.
RT-3  return inside an if (qa is 2): 4, the print and the second return never run.
RT-4  return from two loops deep, at ra 2 rb 2: prints 1 2 3 1, then rx reads 22; the 99 never prints.
RT-5  return with no value after rl = 4. A3 (SEQ 509): on the new road a bare return hands back NOTHING in every
      position, so rx stays 55. The old road hands back the previous statement's value, 4 -- pinned as context, not as
      intended.
RT-5f the bare return as the FIRST statement. New road: nothing (55). Old road: the return keyword node itself, which read
      6 when measured (SEQ 508 R0) -- a value nobody wrote; pinned as context.
RT-5p the bare return after a print. New road: nothing (55). Old road: the print's value, 1 -- context.
RT-6  A4, the no-leak row: the caller's own loop calls a callee that returns, three passes, then prints 8: 1 2 3 8. A signal
      left set after the landing would end the caller's loop after its first pass.
RT-7  a self-call that returns at depth (newLocalT's AL-3 shape, read by value): a declared counter (2) counts down, each
      level returns its own r7 and the level above prints it: 0 1, then rx reads 2. A shared local would print 0 0 / 0.
      ⚠ rt7n is BUILT FIRST, and that is load-bearing: after any new-road body holding a bare call statement (`f(1);`) is
      built, the NEXT new-road build RUNS an assignment's call (`x = f(1);`) at build -- measured SEQ 509, a pre-existing
      gap, reported not chased. Built after rt6n, rt7n ran itself at build, printed 0 1 and spent the counter.
